diff --git a/docs/PRIVACY.md b/docs/PRIVACY.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..322b956
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/PRIVACY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+# Privacy architecture and third-party review
+
+Review date: 2026-10-20. Scope: repository source, production HTML resource references, HTTP security headers, session/API lifecycle, and production dependency advisories. This is an internal technical review, not an independent certification or legal assessment.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+| Data | Purpose | Location | Retention / deletion |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Random session ID | Associate explicitly saved articles with this browser | HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookie, Secure and __Host-prefixed in production | 30-minute cookie; destroyed on clear or removal of last article |
+| Up to 100 article identifiers | Reading list | Bounded in-memory LRU store (1,000 sessions per process) | 30 minutes after last write; automatic expiry purge; immediate session deletion from privacy controls |
+| Search query | Filter public article metadata | React component memory | Component lifecycle; never submitted as a query or stored |
+| Connection information | Deliver HTTP responses | Hosting infrastructure | Operator must document and minimize access-log retention |
+
+Passive browsing and reading-list GET requests do not initialize sessions. No credential, identity, analytics, advertisement, or device fingerprint collection is implemented. Private responses use `Cache-Control: private, no-store`. Request bodies are bounded to 1 KiB, accept only an existing article ID, and reject additional fields. Cross-origin mutations are rejected even for JSON and DELETE.
+
+Memory storage intentionally loses lists on restarts, eviction, or routing to another instance. Before scaling, use a reviewed shared store with automatic TTL deletion, encrypted transport and storage, restricted credentials, and documented backup retention. Do not fall back to indefinite filesystem storage.
+
+## Integration inventory and decisions
+
+| Integration | Previously exposed data | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Cloud Functions analytics script | Page URLs, interaction context, script access to the DOM | Removed; no consent-based loader or tracking SDK remains |
+| Cloud Functions authentication demo | Username and plaintext password payload over HTTPS to a third party | Removed; account forms paused pending provider review and a complete authenticated-session design |
+| Advertising SDK queue | DOM slot references and potential page context | Removed; static layout placeholders remain |
+| Google Fonts | Reader IP, browser metadata, connection timing | Removed; use system serif fallback |
+| Imgur and Cinemai images | Reader IP and image request context | Replaced with existing, locally hosted editorial photography |
+| Hosting platform | IP and HTTP request metadata | Necessary for delivery; deployment owner must review access controls, encryption, logs, region, subprocessors and deletion terms |
+| npm build dependencies | Build-time supply-chain access; no intentional reader disclosure | Lockfile committed; run production dependency advisory audit in CI |
+| External editorial links | Destination sees connection data after a click | Ordinary links only; `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` |
+
+## Review gate for new integrations
+
+The integration proposer owns the initial review. The deployment owner approves activation and records reviewer, date, evidence, decision, and next review date here. Reassess quarterly and whenever an SDK, purpose, data field, vendor, or subprocessor changes.
+
+Before activation, document the exact data and network destinations, necessary purpose, retention and deletion API, data location and transfers, subprocessors, vendor security evidence, incident process, contract terms, and whether the feature can work with less data. Review permissions and cookie behavior. Prefer local resources and static links. Optional embeds require an explicit click before any vendor request. Optional tracking must remain off unless a reviewed consent and withdrawal mechanism exists.
+
+For immutable external scripts, verify SRI and CORS support. Do not use SRI as a substitute for reviewing vendor behavior or silently updated scripts. Add only reviewed destinations to CSP and update regression assertions with review evidence. Never allow arbitrary proxy targets.
+
+A qualified independent reviewer should assess new credential processing or sensitive-data integrations before activation. No independent vendor audit has been commissioned or claimed by this change.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+- `pnpm test:privacy`: builds and starts an isolated production server; checks public/article pages, hashes for every emitted inline script, absence of third-party HTML resources, passive cookie behavior, security headers, malformed/oversized inputs, cross-site mutation rejection, session isolation and deletion. No browser required.
+- `pnpm audit:dependencies`: production supply-chain advisory check. On this review, updated vulnerable transitive Vite to a patched release; no remaining known production advisories.
+
+HTML resource checks do not prove all JavaScript behavior safe; review changed scripts and dependencies as well. Do not upload HAR files or raw reports containing cookies, tokens, URLs with queries, or personal data. There is no telemetry reporting collector, avoiding another source of personal-data retention. Trusted Types enforcement is deferred until Astro/React hydration sinks have been assessed; inline script hashes provide the current CSP boundary.
+
+## Deployment requirements
+
+Serve production exclusively through HTTPS and forward the validated public origin through the trusted proxy configuration. Do not accept arbitrary client-supplied forwarded headers. HSTS is emitted for HTTPS requests; the edge must also enforce HTTPS and apply equivalent headers to static assets and errors it serves outside Astro middleware. Enable encrypted disks and backups at the hosting layer, avoid request-body/cookie/query logging, restrict administrative access, and document infrastructure log retention. Only local `astro dev` uses an unprefixed, non-Secure cookie for HTTP development.
+
+The client router was removed because Astro's CSP implementation does not support it; ordinary page navigation preserves generated script hashes. Keep Astro's generated CSP when adding header directives. Never replace it with a broad script allowlist or `unsafe-inline` for scripts.
